course: dictionary
lesson: 11984
title: Certified: official or completely true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you about proof. «Certified» means someone has officially shown that something is true or meets a set standard. The words around it are clues: a test, a stamp, a judge or a standard will tell you the meaning is official.
explain: «The lifeguard is a certified swimmer, so she passed the official test.» The clue is passed the official test. Certified means officially approved. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The lifeguard is an officially approved swimmer" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Be careful, because certified has a slang meaning too. «He is a certified legend» does not mean he owns a certificate. It means he is truly, completely a legend. Ask yourself: is there an official check, or is the writer just adding emphasis?
explain: The verb certify means to state officially that something is true. So «The judges certified the results» means the judges officially confirmed them. The -ed ending shows this happened in the past, and certified is the past form of certify.
fact: Certified means officially shown to have passed a test or to meet a standard.
fact: «Certified» can also mean officially confirmed as true, as in «a certified copy».
fact: In slang, «a certified genius» means a truly, completely great genius.
fact: Certify is the verb, and certified is its past form: «The judges certified the results.»
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The shop sells certified organic apples.» What does certified mean here? | Covered in mud | *Officially checked to meet an organic standard | Sold very cheaply | Being thrown away
exercise: Dad brings a certified copy of the form to school. Why is the copy certified? | It is the very first copy ever made | *It has been officially confirmed as true | It is a photo of a cat | It is written in pencil
exercise: «The judges certified the results on Monday.» Which word is the past form of certify? | judges | results | Monday | *certified
test: Maya says, «Ben is a certified genius!» What does certified mean here? | Officially passed a genius test | Written down on paper | Hidden away | *Truly, completely
test: «The council certified the building as safe.» What does certified mean? | Painted bright red | *Officially confirmed as true or safe | Left empty for years | Moved to a new place
exam: «The results were certified by the judges.» What does certified mean? | *Officially confirmed as true | Hidden | Lost | Argued about
